<LabLayout title="Expeditions: model & research · Tether Lab" description="How the persistent Earth–Moon–Phobos–Mercury–Ceres campaign works, what it calculates, and which assumptions belong to the game." active="campaign">
  <main id="lab-content" class="guide">
    <header class="guide-header"><p class="micro">EXPEDITIONS / MODEL & RESEARCH</p><h1>A network worth coming back to.</h1><p>Begin with an Earth depot. Deliver construction cargo to the Moon, commission a lunavator, and build a Phobos-anchored transport hub. Then grow a solar swarm from Mercury and supply a water outpost on Ceres through Phobos.</p><div class="guide-meta"><span>CAMPAIGN MODEL {CAMPAIGN_MODEL.replace('network-','')}</span><span>LOCAL SAVES + BACKUPS</span><span>STRATEGIC LOGISTICS</span></div><a class="button-link primary" href="/lab/campaign/">Open your expeditions</a></header>
    <section class="guide-section"><h2>What you can do now</h2><p>Your operations view brings every outpost, the network map, cargo controls and live traffic together. Select a destination to locate it; the other depots stay visible. Supply buttons prepare a shipment for that outpost. The arrival queue includes both cargo and mirror deployments, sorted by arrival. The clock remains available as you scroll. Press Space to pause or resume while viewing the map or panels. Holding the key toggles once. When a field, button, link or disclosure has keyboard focus, Space keeps its normal editing or activation behavior. Use the milestone prompt for your next goal and the milestones disclosure to review completed chapters.</p><p>Use bootstrap tugs to deliver cargo to unbuilt depots. Thirty tonnes of delivered construction cargo commissions a tier-one facility. When both ends have a tether, book the corridor service. Its capacity is limited by the lower-rated endpoint. Upgrade facilities using materials delivered to their own depots.</p><p>Earth–Moon, Earth–Phobos and direct Moon–Phobos corridors support travel in both directions. Construction material and equipment are separate cargo types, each stored at its destination. Each delivery is credited exactly once. Construction consumes cargo; launching reserves cargo and support propellant immediately. Earth supplies remain available every 30 simulation days, so an empty depot is recoverable.</p><p>Advance by a day, 30 days, or the next event; or press Play at 1, 10 or 30 simulation days per second. Production, arrivals and scheduled departures are processed in time order even during a large jump. Dispatching cargo, building facilities, changing services and saving keep Play running; the next automatic step waits until the action is saved. The manual day and next-event buttons pause Play before advancing. Hiding or closing the tab pauses the campaign. Reloading, changing worlds and a failed save also stop automatic play. There is no offline progress or real-time waiting requirement.</p></section>
    <section class="guide-section"><h2>Give each destination a job.</h2><p>Earth starts with manufacturing, 20 t of equipment, and its existing construction and propellant stocks. Manufacturing contributes 0.5 t of equipment and 1 t of pooled support propellant per simulation day. Earth-surface resources and delivery into the network are abstracted.</p><p>Install a lunar processor or a Phobos staging depot for 20 t construction material and 5 t equipment at that location, after commissioning its tether. The processor produces 1 t construction material per day and consumes 0.05 t equipment per day as maintenance. The staging depot consumes 0.5 t construction material and 0.02 t equipment per day to earn one Mars operations point. An operations point is an abstract measure of supported activity, not a simulated surface mission or scientific quantity.</p><p>Production stops when an input is exhausted or output storage is full. Inputs are consumed only for completed production. Incoming deliveries reserve storage; each resource has a limit of one million tonnes per depot. Early industry uses its baseline capacity; Chapter 06 adds paid Ceres and Phobos capacity expansions.</p><p>For a working supply loop, schedule 5 t equipment from Earth to the Moon every 90 days, 3 t equipment from Earth to Phobos every 100 days, and 10 t lunar construction material to Phobos every 20 days. Both remote industries need their initial construction and equipment deliveries first. The first Phobos deliveries take months of simulation time.</p></section>
    <section class="guide-section"><h2>Services that wait for their cargo.</h2><p>Use the flight form to choose origin, destination, cargo type, payload and transport, then schedule an interval from 1 to 3,650 days. The first attempt is the next simulation day. A successful departure schedules the next one after that interval. If supplies, endpoint capacity, recovery, propellant, storage or traffic limits block a departure, the service tries again one day later. Missed departures do not accumulate.</p><p>Up to twelve services can share a network. At the same instant, deliveries arrive before service bookings, and older service IDs get priority. Every departure uses the same cargo, fuel, capacity and recovery rules as a manual flight. Edit a service to change its payload or interval while preserving its history, next attempt and flights already travelling. The new interval applies after its next successful departure. Pause or remove a service to stop new attempts; flights already in transit still arrive. Resuming schedules the next attempt no earlier than tomorrow. Next event includes service attempts, arrivals, Mercury and belt production cycles, mirror launches, recovery and supply availability.</p><p>Track cargo or a mirror launch from the traffic list to highlight its corridor and marker. The fixed panel under the map shows its payload, destination and remaining time, then reports delivery or deployment. Construction, equipment, water and mirrors have distinct marker shapes and colors. On phones, tracking brings this panel into view. Returned power and Mercury’s capacity multiplier also appear beside the map title; select the readout to open the power controls. Marker position is elapsed fraction along a diagram line, not a spacecraft position. Free rotors orbit Earth, the Moon, Mercury and Ceres; Phobos and its anchored arms orbit Mars together. Swarm rings move around the Sun. These animations have illustrative periods and fixed schematic corridor endpoints; they do not calculate encounters. They run with Play, hold their pose on Pause, and stop entirely under reduced motion.</p></section>
    <section class="guide-section" id="lunavator"><h2>The Moon has its own machine.</h2><p>A lunavator is a rotating tether in lunar orbit. It is not attached to the lunar surface. The historical Cislunar Tether Transport System studies paired an Earth tether with a lunar rotor to exchange cargo with the Moon; lunar pickup and delivery depend on the tether's orbit, spin, and rendezvous geometry.</p><p>In this chapter, the lunavator is a constructible transport facility with a finite service rating and recovery schedule. Its surface pickup, flexible dynamics, capture shock, and orbital momentum exchange are not numerically integrated. An operational game route is not a validated reproduction of the historical design.</p><p><a href="https://www.niac.usra.edu/files/studies/final_report/7Hoyt.pdf">Hoyt, Cislunar Tether Transport System, NIAC Phase I final report (1999)</a> — project summary, section III.A.3 and Appendix B.</p></section>
    <section class="guide-section" id="phobos"><h2>Phobos is the central anchor.</h2><p>The campaign's architecture has an inward tether facing Mars and another extending outward from Phobos. The moon itself anchors the structure. This is distinct from the free rotor used at Earth and from the lunar lunavator.</p><p>This is a logistics representation of a proposed architecture. The model does not calculate anchor strength, excavation, tether tension, flexible motion, or Mars-surface rendezvous. Arrival at the Phobos depot is a scheduled handling event; it is not a solved interception or landing trajectory.</p><p><a href="https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20030065879">Weinstein, Space Colonization Using Space-Elevators from Phobos (2003)</a> — conceptual architecture reference, not a hardware qualification.</p></section>
    <section class="guide-section"><h2>Transfer times have a physical basis.</h2><p>Coast time is calculated as half the period of an ideal transfer ellipse: π × √(a³ / μ), where a is the average of the departure and arrival orbital radii. This is a two-body, circular-orbit estimate. It does not use live planetary positions, launch windows, inclination changes, finite burns, or three-body capture dynamics.</p><ul><li>Earth–Moon: Earth gravitational parameter 3.986004418 × 10¹⁴ m³/s², Earth radius 6,371 km, departure altitude 1,600 km, and lunar orbital radius 384,400 km. Coast estimate: {ROUTES[0].coastDays.toFixed(2)} days, plus one scenario day for handling.</li><li>Earth–Phobos: solar gravitational parameter 1.32712440018 × 10²⁰ m³/s², Earth at 1 AU, and Mars at 1.523679 AU. Coast estimate: {ROUTES[1].coastDays.toFixed(2)} days, plus three scenario days for Mars-system handling. Phobos interception is not calculated.</li><li>Moon–Phobos: the same heliocentric coast estimate, approximating the Moon at Earth’s solar orbital radius, plus four scenario days for departure and arrival handling. It does not calculate escape from lunar orbit or an encounter with Phobos. Reverse routes use the same estimates.</li><li>Mercury supply: Mercury is fixed at 0.38709927 AU, using the JPL table-one semimajor axis. Earth–Mercury and Moon–Mercury have an ideal coast of {ROUTES[3].coastDays.toFixed(2)} days plus four and five handling days respectively. Phobos–Mercury approximates departure at Mars’s solar radius, with {ROUTES[5].coastDays.toFixed(2)} coast days plus six handling days. These corridors also work in reverse.</li><li>Phobos–Ceres: Ceres uses a frozen 2.8 AU circular radius from <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/dwarf-planets/ceres/facts/">NASA’s rounded mean-distance reference</a>, with Phobos at the existing Mars radius. The ideal coast is {ROUTES[6].coastDays.toFixed(2)} days plus six handling days in either direction. Ceres connects only through Phobos.</li></ul><p><a href="https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/astro_par.html">JPL astrodynamic parameters</a> and <a href="https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/planets/approx_pos.html">approximate planetary elements</a> provide the reference context. The scenario deliberately freezes its constants; these are not ephemerides.</p></section>
    <section class="guide-section"><h2>Game assumptions, openly stated</h2><p>These values define the playable network. They are balancing choices, not engineering predictions:</p><ul><li>Construction: 30 / 60 / 90 t for tiers 1 / 2 / 3, built immediately.</li><li>Service rating: 10 / 20 / 30 t per tether flight; bootstrap tugs carry 10 t.</li><li>Recovery: a network booking reserves both endpoint services for 2 / tier simulation days. It is a simplified scheduling rule, including the destination reservation, not an arrival-time rendezvous solver.</li><li>Support propellant: 0.35 t per cargo tonne for cislunar tugs and 1.2 t per cargo tonne for either Mars corridor’s tugs. Mercury tugs allocate 1.8 t per cargo tonne from Earth or the Moon, or 2.2 t from Phobos. Phobos–Ceres tugs allocate 0.5 t per cargo tonne. Tether bookings use 40% of these allocations. This is a network budget for assumed targeting, handling and recovery support; no rocket equation or quantified historical tether fuel saving is implied.</li><li>Supply: 60 t construction cargo at Earth and 60 t support propellant per allocation, available every 30 days. Support propellant is pooled across the network; its own delivery is abstracted.</li><li>Up to 256 active cargo flights plus 128 mirror batches in transit, with independent limits so swarm growth leaves room for supply lines, 12 save slots and 100,000 simulation days per campaign.</li></ul><p>The Flight Studio offers separate <a href="/lab/">Earth</a>, <a href="/lab/lunar/">lunar</a> and <a href="/lab/phobos/">Phobos anchored experiments</a>. Campaign facility ratings are not imported from a Studio design. Successful Studio runs do not validate campaign routes, surface pickup or arriving-cargo capture.</p></section>
    <section class="guide-section"><h2>Saves that protect your progress</h2><p>Open Your saves for manual saving, backups, imports and recovery. Every successful action, including an automatic time step, attempts a browser save. The screen reports success only after the database transaction completes. Each named campaign retains its three preceding checkpoints. Recovering a checkpoint or importing a backup creates a separate slot, preserving the original.</p><p>Saves belong to this browser and site address. A preview address and the main website have separate storage. Download a backup before switching devices, changing site addresses, using private browsing, or clearing browser data. Browser storage can also be removed under storage pressure. There is no account or cloud synchronisation in this chapter.</p><p>If two tabs edit the same save, the older tab is prevented from overwriting newer progress. Keep an export of the older tab if you want that branch, then load the latest saved world. First-chapter saves (schema 1, network-0.1.0) upgrade compatibly when loaded: simulation day, existing stocks, facility tiers, flights and arrival dates, logs and objectives are preserved. Earth receives the new manufacturing facility and a one-time 20 t equipment allocation; no past production is added. The first write retains the old state as a recovery checkpoint and advances its revision, including when using Save now. Working-network saves (schema 2, network-0.2.0) retain their equipment, industries, schedules, active flights and all existing progress; they receive no additional Earth allocation or past production. Mercury begins unbuilt and locked. First-light saves (schema 3, network-0.3.0) preserve every existing stock, facility, clock, service and in-flight deployment. The new power link starts unbuilt; there is no past production or free allocation. You can continue a completed save. Power-loop saves (schema 4, network-0.4.0) preserve every existing field, including connected power, production and deployment clocks. All earlier worlds gain an empty Ceres port, zero water stocks and a locked belt expedition, with no past extraction or free allocation. Backups from versions one through five still import into new slots. New exports use version six (network-0.6.0), including industrial upgrades, purchased mining tracts and the automatic-launch fuel reserve. Existing version-five saves retain every resource, flight, service and clock; the new development fields start at zero, granting no upgrades or past production. Existing version-five saves from network-0.5.0 gain the expanded, independent flight limits while retaining every resource, arrival date, schedule and field of progress. There is no need to restart. The first write keeps the old head as a checkpoint and advances its revision. Unrecognised versions are rejected without replacing existing campaigns.</p></section>
    <section class="guide-section" id="mercury"><h2>Mercury supplies the first solar swarm.</h2><p>Chapter 03 opens at 100 Mars operations points. The expedition consumes 60 t construction material and 20 t equipment at Earth for surveying and ground support. This does not deliver cargo to Mercury: use bootstrap tugs to establish its depot, then commission a free-orbit rotovator for 30 t construction and a refinery for another 20 t construction plus 5 t equipment.</p><p>The refinery processes a finite, 100,000 t local scenario deposit. Once installed, its first production cycle is one simulation day later, then daily. Each cycle can convert up to 2 t of the deposit into 2 t construction material, consuming 0.1 t equipment. This deposit is a game allocation, not an estimate of Mercury’s reserves or planetary mass.</p><p>Install mirror works at Mercury for 40 t construction material and 10 t equipment. After the refinery runs each day, the works can convert 1 t construction material and 0.05 t equipment into 1 t mirrors. Production uses only available inputs and storage; partial output is possible. The two facilities together need up to 0.15 t equipment per day. After sending the initial building supplies and working stock, an Earth–Mercury service carrying 10 t equipment every 60 days can sustain them. Allow months for the first shipment to arrive.</p><p>A mirror launch array costs another 40 t construction and 10 t equipment and requires the works. The launch button sends 10 t mirrors, consuming 1 t pooled support propellant and reserving the Mercury tether for 2 / tier days. It shares Mercury recovery with cargo services and uses a separate allowance of 128 mirror batches in transit. Cargo has its own 256-flight capacity. Mirrors leave stock at departure and count as deployed only after arrival.</p><p>Automatic launch mode first attempts tomorrow, then every ten days after a successful 10 t launch. A blocked attempt retries tomorrow; missed launches do not build up. Pause stops future attempts while preserving batches already travelling. At any shared event time, cargo deliveries and mirror deployments arrive first, then local tooling (when connected), Mercury’s refinery and works run, then the belt extraction and propellant cycle, then cargo services book in ID order, and finally automatic mirror launch attempts run. Earlier industry production still integrates continuously between events.</p><p>The nominal swarm radius is {SOLAR.radiusAU} AU. Half-period transfer time from Mercury’s frozen radius plus two scenario handling days gives {SOLAR.deploymentDays.toFixed(2)} days to deployment. Capture, targeting, solar radiation pressure, deployment mechanics and thermal survival are not solved. The scenario assumes 10 g/m² of deployed mass, so each tonne adds 0.1 km² of mirror area. That is an area assumption, not a demonstrated complete spacecraft mass budget. The optional power loop below estimates intercepted sunlight and returned power under explicit scenario assumptions. Symbols on the diagram represent batches rather than individual mirrors.</p><p>The four milestones are establishing the refinery, completing the works and array, deploying the first 10 t, and reaching 100 t deployed (10 km² at the assumed mass per area). Existing worlds gain this chapter without resetting earlier progress.</p><p>The specific mirror material remains provisional, including the proposed hematite concept. Mercury’s iron-rich bulk interior does not establish an accessible surface supply of hematite: MESSENGER composition results describe an iron-poor surface and strongly reducing conditions. Refining recipes, coating choices, oxidation, optical performance and durability need further research before becoming hardware claims. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1712.02187">Nittler et al., The Chemical Composition of Mercury (2017)</a> provides the composition context.</p></section>
    <section class="guide-section" id="power"><h2>Power the next expansion.</h2><p>Chapter 04 continues your existing swarm. Once 100 t is deployed, connect swarm power for 60 t construction material and 10 t equipment at Mercury. This one-time upgrade includes an abstract receiver, return link and local tooling. Existing stocks, flights and scheduled attempts keep their values; accelerated production begins at the next daily cycle. Leave some material or equipment for that first cycle.</p><p>Intercepted sunlight is deployed area × solar flux. We freeze the reference at 1,361 W/m² at 1 AU and use inverse-square distance: at the swarm’s 0.5 AU, that is 5,444 W/m². A 100 t swarm has 10 km² under the area assumption and intercepts 54.44 GW. With the link online, an assumed combined capture, conversion and return fraction of 20% delivers 10.888 GW to Mercury. Without the link, returned power is zero. <a href="https://earth.gsfc.nasa.gov/climate/projects/solar-irradiance/science">NASA’s solar irradiance reference</a> and <a href="https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/edu/resources/lesson-plan/calculating-solar-power-in-space/">JPL’s inverse-square explanation</a> support the flux calculation. The return efficiency, illuminated area, receivers and power transmission are scenario assumptions; optical, thermal and power-beaming engineering are not solved. The readout measures instantaneous power, not stored energy.</p><p>Returned power is automatically reinvested. The production multiplier is F = 1 + returned GW / 20, giving refinery capacity of 2F t/day and mirror capacity of F t/day. More mirrors produce more returned power, enabling faster mining and manufacturing and then more mirrors. This is a compounding feedback loop with transfer delays and logistics limits, not unlimited exponential growth.</p><p>Local tooling runs first each day. It converts available construction to equipment at one tonne per tonne, up to 0.15F t per cycle, replenishing at most a two-cycle equipment buffer. It consumes real material and respects storage reserved for incoming deliveries. The refinery then runs, followed by the mirror works, with their existing input recipes. Local tooling lets the loop maintain itself after the initial build. The screen distinguishes production capacity from next-cycle output and reports shortages. The original finite 100,000 t mining tract remains: this is a first Mercury extraction operation, not a whole-planet disassembly calculation.</p><p>Connected automatic launches initially use Mercury’s tier capacity: 10, 20 or 30 t. Chapter 06 mirror-array expansions multiply the mirror batch size while cargo ratings stay at the original tier capacity. Each successful launch schedules the next after the greater of batch mass / F days or the 2 / tier recovery time. Existing attempt dates stay unchanged until that attempt runs. Blocked launches retry tomorrow. Upgrade Mercury’s tether for larger batches; keep support fuel and traffic slots available. Transit still takes about 56 days, and power rises only when a batch arrives. Paused automatic launches stay paused when the link is installed.</p><p>Four new milestones follow First light: connect the power loop, double Mercury capacity with 20 GW returned, reach 100 GW returned, and deploy 2,000 t with the link online. Completed earlier milestones remain completed; there is no need to restart your network.</p></section>
    <section class="guide-section" id="ceres"><h2>Into the Belt: water through the Phobos hub.</h2><p>Chapter 05 continues a completed power-loop world. Connect swarm power, deploy 2,000 t of mirrors, and prepare a tier-two Phobos anchor with its staging depot. Opening Ceres consumes 60 t construction material and 20 t equipment at Phobos. Funding opens the corridor; construction and working supplies must travel separately.</p><p>Send 50 t material to Ceres: 30 t commissions its free-orbit rotovator and 20 t builds water works. The works also needs 5 t equipment to build. An initial 20 t equipment shipment leaves 15 t working stock to cover the long supply lead time. At up to 2 t water per day, extraction consumes 0.01 t equipment per tonne. A 5 t Phobos–Ceres equipment service every 200 days covers that demand once deliveries begin. Keep Phobos supplied from your existing network.</p><p>Install Phobos propellant works for another 40 t local material and 10 t equipment. Return water from Ceres as ordinary cargo. At Phobos, up to 2 t water becomes an equal mass of support fuel per daily cycle, using 0.005 t equipment per tonne. Fuel joins the existing shared pool and can support all corridors and Mercury launches. Water at Ceres or in flight adds no fuel. A 10 t tether return spends 2 t fuel and can yield 10 t at the plant, before equipment shipping and construction costs. Start a return service every 90 days, then expand its payload or frequency as your traffic allows.</p><p>Belt cycles start one day after funding and repeat daily; installed works use the next scheduled cycle. At a shared event time, arrivals credit first, Mercury production runs, then Ceres extraction and Phobos refining, then scheduled cargo and automatic mirror launches. Inputs and free storage limit output. A full fuel pool preserves water and equipment, and partial cycles spend only the inputs they use. The 100,000 t local water deposit is finite. Saved water is accounted for across the remaining deposit, depots, cargo in transit and processed fuel.</p><p>Four milestones follow the expedition: fund Ceres, establish its water works, process the first 10 t at Phobos, then process 100 t with a water return service that has made at least three departures and one delivery. These milestones extend existing saves without resetting them.</p><p><a href="https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/where-is-the-ice-on-ceres-new-nasa-dawn-findings/">NASA Dawn findings on Ceres ice</a> provide the scientific context. The allocated deposit, extraction rates, equipment use and one-to-one water-to-propellant conversion are scenario assumptions. Power, heat rejection, electrolysis, cryogenic storage, surface access and losses are not modeled. This is a supply-chain game, with an assumed orbiting port, not a validated Ceres mine or Flight Studio design.</p></section>
    <section class="guide-section" id="development"><h2>Industrial scale: choose the next bottleneck.</h2><p>Chapter 06 opens after swarm power is linked, Ceres water extraction and Phobos propellant works are installed, and 100 t of returned water has been refined. Continue your existing save. Choose where to invest local construction and equipment: expand mirror launch throughput, extract more Ceres water, or refine more fuel at Phobos. The chapter culminates in 50,000 t deployed with all three industries expanded.</p><p>Each facility has three expansion levels. The first Mercury array expansion costs 300 t construction and 20 t equipment at Mercury; subsequent material costs triple and equipment costs double. Each level doubles the mirror batch capacity, reaching 240 t with a tier-three Mercury port and three array expansions. Support propellant remains 0.1 t per mirror tonne and recovery remains 2/tier days. Cargo capacity is unchanged. These are gameplay throughput ratings, not demonstrated tether or launch hardware.</p><p>Ceres water works and Phobos propellant works each cost 100 t material and 10 t equipment for the first expansion, with both costs doubling for each later level. Daily capacities rise from 2 to 4, 8 and 16 t. Equipment use per tonne is unchanged, and actual output still requires available inputs and storage. Extracting faster requires more equipment deliveries and water transport; a larger Phobos plant still needs water that has actually arrived.</p><p>Protect fuel for cargo by setting a reserve. Automatic mirror attempts run after scheduled cargo and wait if the launch would leave less fuel than the reserve. They retry tomorrow. Cargo and explicit manual mirror launches may spend that reserve. Changing the setting or buying a project never rewrites an existing flight or pending attempt date.</p><p>Additional mining tracts keep mature saves growing after the initial reserves run out. Each site allows eight paid 100,000 t tracts. Mercury tract n costs 500n t material and 20n t equipment locally; Ceres tract n costs 150n and 10n. Purchasing access adds only unprocessed deposit, which must pass through the normal extraction chain. These finite allocations are scenario choices, not estimates of accessible planetary reserves. The water ledger includes every purchased Ceres tract.</p><p>Use Network outlook to project 30, 90 or 365 days with the current services and automatic launches. It reports depot stocks, fuel, mirror deployments and the exact reasons for blocked service or automatic mirror attempts without changing your save. Holds are grouped by cause and link to the affected controls. It assumes no manual shipments, upgrades or Earth allocations. Try a service’s payload and interval in the outlook first: it compares that service’s departures and holds, total cargo received, support fuel and mirror launches using the same forecast window. This comparison does not change your save; apply the schedule only when you choose. The service’s next attempt and cargo already in flight keep their dates. The map adds infrastructure indicators for upgrades and schematic swarm bands at 2,000, 10,000 and 50,000 t deployed.</p></section>
    <section class="guide-section"><h2>Where the network can grow next</h2><p>More destinations, lunar surface pickup, Phobos capture and orbit recovery, real launch windows and detailed swarm engineering remain future work. A separate Phobos Studio experiment now explores static cable loads and local releases; the campaign already includes an assumed solar power feedback loop. Your present network can keep supplying industry and expanding its saved swarm.</p><a class="button-link primary" href="/lab/campaign/">Return to your network</a></section>
  </main>
